<meta http-equiv="content-type" content="text/html; charset=utf-8"><div><div><div><div><div><div><div><div><div><div><div><div><div><div><div><div><div><div><div><div>Yeah that's what I thought of... because it was a headache to have internet access when connected to corporate vpn internet is essentially blocked. First I came up with this idea. use one container with openconnect and use another container having the same network with openssh clients so basically I did podman exec into the second container and then ssh pretty rudimentary then I discovered warpgate and welp It solved substantial problems of me being a serverless bastion</div></div></div></div></div></div><div><div><div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div><span></span><div><div><div><span></span><div><div><div></div><div><div><p>LMAO. 💀 You essentially reinvented a <span><strong>client-side VPN gateway architecture</strong></span>, starting with nothing more than Podman, OpenConnect, and an SSH client.</p><p>And the evolution of your solution makes perfect sense.</p><div><ol><li><div><div><p><span><strong>Stage 1: Corporate VPN ruins your internet</strong></span></p><p>Connect OpenConnect on the host, and corporate VPN policies or routes make ordinary internet access unusable.</p><p>Your requirement: keep corporate connectivity without sacrificing your host's internet.</p></div></div></li><li><div><div><p><span><strong>Stage 2: Isolate OpenConnect inside a container</strong></span></p><p>You realized Linux network namespaces could contain the VPN tunnel and its routing changes.</p><p>The host stays outside that namespace, so its routes remain untouched.</p></div></div></li><li><div><div><p><span><strong>Stage 3: Add an SSH client container</strong></span></p><p>You attached another container to the VPN container's network namespace, then used <code>podman exec</code> to run SSH commands.</p><p>Rudimentary? Yes. Architecturally sound? Also yes.</p></div></div></li><li><div><div><p><span><strong>Stage 4: Discover Warpgate</strong></span></p><p>Instead of entering a container to run SSH every time, you deployed Warpgate inside the same namespace.</p><p>Now your host connects to a local SSH/HTTP bastion, which forwards authenticated sessions to corporate resources.</p></div></div></li><li><div><div><p><span><strong>Stage 5: Add a browser inside the VPN</strong></span></p><p>Finally, you wanted to access internal web consoles without configuring each endpoint individually in Warpgate.</p><p>The AI added containerized Firefox with KasmVNC, turning a browser tab on your host into a window onto the VPN-connected browser.</p></div></div></li></ol></div><p>What's particularly neat is the distinction between <span><strong>network access and network isolation</strong></span>.</p><p>You didn't try to make the corporate VPN coexist nicely with your host's networking. You simply stopped allowing the corporate VPN to control the host's networking in the first place.</p><p>That's a much cleaner separation.</p><p>And Warpgate is an especially appropriate discovery for your setup because it's an application-layer bastion rather than a full network tunnel into your host. You can expose specific SSH and HTTP access paths without importing the corporate routing table into the host namespace.</p><p>The phrase <span><em>serverless bastion</em></span> is amusingly accurate in the everyday sense: you didn't need to provision a dedicated bastion VM, maintain another server, or persuade some network administrator to give you a new gateway.</p><p>Technically, Warpgate is still running as a server process, just locally in a container.</p><p>There is one security distinction worth emphasizing: <span><strong>the corporate VPN is network-isolated from the host, but it isn't necessarily fully security-isolated.</strong></span> Published ports, privileged SELinux labels, bind mounts, and routing or DNS leaks can create paths across that boundary. Your design solves the routing conflict; it doesn't automatically guarantee a hard security boundary.</p><p>Still, there's something deeply satisfying about taking the one thing that makes your corporate VPN unbearable, its routing policy, and containing it within a network namespace.</p><p>Instead of fighting your host's routing table, you gave the VPN its own little world to ruin. Beautiful. 💀</p></div></div></div><div></div></div></div></div></div><span></span></div></div><div><div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div><div><div><div><div><div></div></div></div></div></div></div>